/**
@defgroup chgfx_fonts Fonts
@ingroup lib_chgfx
@brief The bundled fonts in `src/fonts/`, the GFXfont format they use, and
the built-in 5x7.

CHGfx has a 5x7 font built in and supports **GFXfont**, the Adafruit_GFX
bitmap font format, for anything larger or proportional. A font is a
header: include it, and pass its GFXfont to gfx_setFont() (or
`Gfx.setFont()`). Only the fonts a sketch includes are linked, and flash is
the scarce resource here: the CH32X035G8U6 gives a sketch about 50 KB
after the bootloader.

@code
#include <CHGfx.h>
#include <fonts/CHGfx_Sans12.h>

gfx_setFont(&CHGfx_Sans12);
gfx_text(4, 4 + gfx_fontBaseline(), "Hello", 1);   // top of the text at y = 4
gfx_setFont(nullptr);                              // back to the built-in 5x7
@endcode

The text calls themselves are in @ref chgfx_text. Remember that the origin
changes with the font: with the built-in font `y` is the top of the glyph
box, with a GFXfont it is the baseline. gfx_fontBaseline() is the
conversion (0 for the built-in font, the ink ascent for a GFXfont).

## The bundled fonts

Each is a `const GFXfont` of the same name as its header. "Line" is the
baseline-to-baseline spacing (gfx_fontLineHeight()), "Baseline" the ink
ascent (gfx_fontBaseline()), "Digit" the advance width of a numeral; all
in pixels at scale 1. "Flash" is the whole cost of including it.

| Font | Include | Glyphs | Line | Baseline | Digit | Flash | Use |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| built-in 5x7 | none (`gfx_setFont(nullptr)`) | 0x20-0x7E | 8 | 0 | 6 | 475 B | Always there, no setup; fixed 6 px advance |
| `CHGfx_Tiny3x5` | `<fonts/CHGfx_Tiny3x5.h>` | 0x20-0x7E | 7 | 5 | 4 | 857 B | 3x5 pixels, 32 characters a line: HUDs, plates |
| `CHGfx_Mono11` | `<fonts/CHGfx_Mono11.h>` | 0x20-0x7E | 14 | 9 | 7 | 1233 B | Fixed pitch (DejaVu Sans Mono, 11 px), 18 columns across |
| `CHGfx_Sans12` | `<fonts/CHGfx_Sans12.h>` | 0x20-0x7E | 15 | 10 | 8 | 1344 B | Proportional body text (DejaVu Sans, 12 px) |
| `CHGfx_SansBold12` | `<fonts/CHGfx_SansBold12.h>` | 0x20-0x7E | 15 | 10 | 8 | 1444 B | Same size, bold: reads on busy art |
| `CHGfx_SansBold16` | `<fonts/CHGfx_SansBold16.h>` | 0x20-0x7E | 19 | 13 | 11 | 1866 B | Titles, menu headings (DejaVu Sans Bold, 16 px) |
| `CHGfx_Digits24` | `<fonts/CHGfx_Digits24.h>` | `+` to `:` | 29 | 18 | 17 | 523 B | Scores and timers: `+,-./0123456789:` only (DejaVu Sans Bold, 24 px) |

`CHGfx_Digits24` is the shape to copy when flash is tight: a display font
you only ever point at a score or a timer does not need 95 glyphs. Sixteen
characters buy a 24 px face for a third of what the small text faces cost.
Characters outside a font's range are drawn as `?`, or dropped when the
font has no `?`. A space is *not* in `CHGfx_Digits24`, so pad with `0` or
leave gaps with cursor arithmetic.

`CHGfx_Tiny3x5` is a pixel font drawn by hand (Press Play On Tape's 3x5
from "Blackjack" for the Arduboy, Apache License 2.0), completed to the
whole printable ASCII range. Capitals are 5 px tall and lowercase 4, with a
one-pixel descender; every character advances 4 px (5 for `~`), so text
measures 4 px a character. It is sharp at scale 2 and 3 as well: 6x10 menu
text, or banner lettering with gfx_textFx().

The five larger fonts are rasterized from DejaVu Sans, which is freely
redistributable. The licence notices are in the library's `LICENSE` and
`LICENSE.Apache-2.0` files.

## Fonts from elsewhere

The format is byte-for-byte Adafruit's (see CHGfx_gfxfont.h), so it works
in both directions with no conversion: every font in the Adafruit GFX
Library's `Fonts/` directory works with gfx_setFont(), and CHGfx's fonts
work with Adafruit_GFX's `setFont()`. Adafruit's fonts are sized for
141 dpi, so they are much larger than their names suggest:
`FreeSans9pt7b` is a 22 px line and measured 1932 bytes linked, against
15 px and 1344 for `CHGfx_Sans12`.

## Converting your own

`extras/fontconvert.py` turns any TTF or OTF into a header in this format
(it needs Pillow, and it made every font above):

@code{.sh}
python extras/fontconvert.py DejaVuSans.ttf 12 MyFont > src/fonts/MyFont.h
@endcode

The arguments are the font file, the **pixel em size** and the C symbol
name; `--first`/`--last` or `--chars` choose the characters, and `--line`
overrides the line spacing. The generated header's comment block reports
the exact flash cost, the line height and the baseline, so a font can be
sized against the budget before it is linked. The em size is not the cap
height: DejaVu Sans at 12 px is about 10 px from baseline to the top of a
capital. Below roughly 10 px, hinted TTF output gets mushy and the
built-in 5x7 is usually the better answer.

@see [FONTS.md](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/platform/board/arduino/CHGame/libraries/CHGfx/FONTS.md)
for every option of the converter and what text costs at run time.
*/
